\begin_layout Standard
Depending upon the plasma regime and the spatial localization, magnetic
 or density fluctuation spectra may be well approximated by Gaussian or
 Cauchy-Lorentz distributions.
 For this purpose, appropriate random numbers must be generated with the
 corresponding probability distribution function (pdf).
 Let denotes the uniform pdf for a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p\left(x\right)=\begin{cases}
1, & 0<x<1\\
0, & otherwise\end{cases}\label{eq:uniformdeviate}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and a non-uniform pdf for the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(y\right)dy$
\end_inset

 which related to 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)dx$
\end_inset

 by the fundamental transformation law of probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left|p\left(y\right)dy\right|=\left|p\left(x\right)dx\right|\label{eq:-1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p\left(y\right)=\left|\frac{dx}{dy}\right|p\left(x\right)\label{eq:py}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $y\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is explicitely known, then it is possible to generate a random numbers
 with a pdf 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 from a the uniform one 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 by applying the transformation 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
y=F^{-1}\left(x\right)\label{eq:invF}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{-1}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 is the inverse of the indefinite integral of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(y\right)=dx/dy$
\end_inset

.
 Wether (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:invF"

\end_inset

) is feasible to implement depends on wether the inverse function of the
 integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

is itself feasible to compute either analytically or numerically.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is nothing but the cumulative distribution function (cdf) associated to
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Cauchy-Lorentz distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the Cauchy-Lorentz distribution centered around 
\begin_inset Formula $y=0$
\end_inset

, the cdf takes the simple form 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pre92"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
F\left(y\right)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{2y}{\Delta}\right)\label{eq:cumcauchylorentzdist}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is the full width at half maximum (FWHM).
 It's inverse value is then
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
F^{-1}\left(x\right)=\frac{\Delta}{2}\tan\left(\pi\left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right)\label{eq:invcumcauchylorentzdist}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the calculation of random numbers with a Cauchy-Lorentz pdf from a uniform
 random generator is straightforward.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting distribution is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p\left(y\right)=\frac{1}{\pi}\frac{\Delta/2}{y^{2}+\left(\Delta/2\right)^{2}}\label{eq:cauchylorentzdist}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Gaussian distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a one dimensional Gaussian or normal distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p\left(y\right)=\frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{y^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\label{eq:gaussiandist}\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\left]-\infty,+\infty\right[$
\end_inset

, the approach is more complex, since 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{-1}$
\end_inset

 does not exist analytically.
 In (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gaussiandist"

\end_inset

), the variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 is related to the full width at half maximum by the usual relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=2\sqrt{2\ln2}\sigma$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of considering the usual Box-Muller method for a two dimensional
 problem 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pre92"

\end_inset

, an efficient standard rejection technique is applied in a first step to
 the normalized Gaussian distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{+}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 on the restricted domain 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\left[0,+\infty\right[$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
p^{+}\left(y\right)=\frac{1}{\sigma}\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{y^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\label{eq:halfgaussiandist}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the pdf (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:halfgaussiandist"

\end_inset

) may be expressed in the form
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
p^{+}\left(y\right) & =\frac{1}{\sigma}\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\exp\left(\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{y}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{\left(y-1\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\label{eq:haldgaussianpdfbis}\end{align}

\end_inset

it is possible to apply the rejection technique but for random numbers which
 are non uniformly distributed 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kah54"

\end_inset

.
 Indeed, the pdf (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:haldgaussianpdfbis"

\end_inset

) may be interpreted as a two dimensional probability distribution
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\widetilde{p}^{+}\left(y,z\right)\equiv\exp\left(-\frac{y}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{z}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\label{eq:2dgaussiandist}\end{equation}

\end_inset

with the usual constraint of acceptance for the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(y,z\right)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pre92"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
-\frac{z}{\sigma^{2}}<-\frac{\left(y-1\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\label{eq:gaussianacceptance}\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\left(y-1\right)^{2}}{2}<z\label{eq:gaussianacceptancebis}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to note that the normalisation factors in front of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:haldgaussianpdfbis"

\end_inset

) do not play a role in the shape of the distribution.
 So they do not contribute to the generation of random numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 with a Gaussian distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rejection procedure starts with the generation of two random numbers
 
\begin_inset Formula $y\left[z\right]$
\end_inset

 whose pdf has the same form 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp\left(-y\left[z\right]/\sigma^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[y\left[z\right],y\left[z\right]+dy\left[dz\right]\right]$
\end_inset

, according to (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:2dgaussiandist"

\end_inset

).
 This is achieved by the relation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
y\left[z\right]=-\sigma^{2}\ln\left(x_{y}\left[x_{z}\right]\right)\label{eq:expgenerator}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{y}\left[x_{z}\right]\in\left]0,1\right[$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pre92"

\end_inset

.
 Once 
\begin_inset Formula $y\left[z\right]$
\end_inset

 have been obtained, the value 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is accepted if the condition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gaussianacceptancebis"

\end_inset

) is fullfiled.
 Otherwise the whole procedure is repeated until the value 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is accepted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The probability of obtaining a satisfactory random number 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on the first trial, which called the efficiency 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}$
\end_inset

 of the random generator, is the sum of the probabilities of selecting a
 value 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 in any one of the separate interval 
\begin_inset Formula $dy$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\mathcal{E} & =\intop_{0}^{+\infty}\exp\left(-\frac{y}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{\left(y-1\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)dy\nonumber \\
 & =\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\label{eq:efficiency}\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}\simeq0.76$
\end_inset

, which is a high number for a rejection method.
 However, as shown in Fig.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FigGaussianGenEfficiency"

\end_inset

, the performances of the Gaussian random generator degradate rapidly when
 the variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 is less than unity, otherwise, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}$
\end_inset

 scales almost linearly with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

